{# magic-link.v1 — TRANSACTIONAL sign-in link (#2272).
   payload = { link, expires_minutes }

   Three ways this message differs from every other template here, each deliberate:

   1. NO UNSUBSCRIBE FOOTER. Every notification template ends with one; this must not. You cannot
      unsubscribe from being able to log in, and an auth envelope carries no `consent_snapshot` and
      therefore no `unsubscribe_ref` — so a footer here would render a dead link into a real inbox.
   2. NO GRAPH CHIPS. The other templates carry corpus content and the moat rule applies to them.
      This message is about the recipient's own session, not about the corpus.
   3. ONE ACTION, AND IT IS A SECRET. The link is the credential while it lives. The copy says how
      long that is, and says plainly what to do if they did not ask for it — because an unexpected
      sign-in email is the one signal a person gets that someone typed their address.

   Email-client-SAFE, like its siblings: dark background on a wrapper table rather than <body>. #}
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1"></head>
<body style="margin:0;padding:0;background-color:#0b0e14;">
<table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#0b0e14" style="background-color:#0b0e14;">
  <tr><td align="center" style="padding:32px 16px;">
    <table role="presentation" width="600" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" bgcolor="#0b0e14" style="width:600px;max-width:600px;background-color:#0b0e14;font-family:-apple-system,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',Roboto,Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;">
      <tr><td style="padding:0 8px;">
        <div style="letter-spacing:.14em;font-size:12px;text-transform:uppercase;color:#8aa0bd;">Close Listening</div>

        <p style="color:#e7ecf3;font-size:22px;font-weight:600;margin:14px 0 6px;">Sign in to Close Listening</p>
        <p style="color:#c3ccd9;font-size:15px;line-height:1.5;margin:0 0 24px;">
          Tap the button below and you are in. No password to remember.
        </p>

        {# The button is a table so Outlook renders it; the bare <a> underneath is the fallback for
           clients that strip button styling, and for anyone who prefers to see the URL. #}
        <table role="presentation" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" style="margin:0 0 20px;">
          <tr><td bgcolor="#e7ecf3" style="border-radius:999px;">
            <a href="{{ payload.link }}" style="display:inline-block;padding:13px 28px;font-size:16px;font-weight:600;color:#0b0e14;text-decoration:none;border-radius:999px;">Sign in</a>
          </td></tr>
        </table>

        <p style="color:#8aa0bd;font-size:13px;line-height:1.5;margin:0 0 6px;">
          This link works once and expires in {{ payload.expires_minutes | default(15) }} minutes.
        </p>
        <p style="color:#6b7a90;font-size:12px;line-height:1.5;margin:0 0 24px;word-break:break-all;">
          If the button does not work, paste this into your browser:<br>
          <a href="{{ payload.link }}" style="color:#8aa0bd;">{{ payload.link }}</a>
        </p>

        <table role="presentation" width="100%" cellpadding="0" cellspacing="0" style="border-top:1px solid #1c2636;margin-top:8px;"><tr><td style="padding-top:20px;color:#6b7a90;font-size:12px;">
          <p style="margin:0;color:#6b7a90;">
            You are receiving this because someone asked to sign in with this address.
            If that was not you, ignore this email — the link expires on its own and nothing happens.
          </p>
        </td></tr></table>
      </td></tr>
    </table>
  </td></tr>
</table>
</body>
</html>
